{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzhen","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Yuzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuangxue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuangxue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MSCoRe: A Benchmark for Multi-Stage Collaborative Reasoning in LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T11:36:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have excelled in question-answering (QA) tasks within single domains. However, their reasoning and coordination capabilities in complex, multi-stage scenarios remain underexplored. Existing benchmarks typically focus on isolated tasks or narrow domains, overlooking models' abilities for multi-stage collaboration and optimization without explicit external guidance. To bridge this gap, we propose \\textbf{MSCoRe}, a novel benchmark comprising 126696 domain-specific QA instances spanning scenarios in automotive, pharmaceutical, electronics, and energy sectors. The dataset is created using a structured three-phase pipeline: dynamic sampling, iterative question-answer generation, and a multi-level quality assessment to ensure data quality. Tasks are further categorized into three difficulty levels according to stage coverage and complexity. With MSCoRe, we have conducted a comprehensive evaluation of various state-of-the-art LLM agents. The commercial models performed best across all tasks and scenarios, but a notable gap in ROUGE scores remains between simple and complex tasks. We also tested the models' robustness and found that their performance is negatively affected by noisy data. MSCoRe provides a valuable new resource for the community to evaluate and improve multi-stage reasoning in LLM agents. The code and data are available at https://github.com/D3E0-source/MSCoRE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:16:31Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:16:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:33:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Aiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tianyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanming","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yirun","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Yirun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihe","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhihe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COLA: Context-aware Language-driven Test-time Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T11:19:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:42:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tip.2025.3607634","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time adaptation (TTA) has gained increasing popularity due to its efficacy in addressing ``distribution shift'' issue while simultaneously protecting data privacy.\n However, most prior methods assume that a paired source domain model and target domain sharing the same label space coexist, heavily limiting their applicability.\n In this paper, we investigate a more general source model capable of adaptation to multiple target domains without needing shared labels.\n This is achieved by using a pre-trained vision-language model (VLM), \\egno, CLIP, that can recognize images through matching with class descriptions.\n While the zero-shot performance of VLMs is impressive, they struggle to effectively capture the distinctive attributes of a target domain.\n To that end, we propose a novel method -- Context-aware Language-driven TTA (COLA).\n The proposed method incorporates a lightweight context-aware module that consists of three key components: a task-aware adapter, a context-aware unit, and a residual connection unit for exploring task-specific knowledge, domain-specific knowledge from the VLM and prior knowledge of the VLM, respectively.\n It is worth noting that the context-aware module can be seamlessly integrated into a frozen VLM, ensuring both minimal effort and parameter efficiency.\n Additionally, we introduce a Class-Balanced Pseudo-labeling (CBPL) strategy to mitigate the adverse effects caused by class imbalance.\n We demonstrate the effectiveness of our method not only in TTA scenarios but also in class generalisation tasks.\n The source code is available at https://github.com/NUDT-Bai-Group/COLA-TTA."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:15:49Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:15:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:33:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xia","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaoxin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yaoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minshuo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiguang","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Zhiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingqian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yingqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models as End-to-end Combinatorial Optimization Solvers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T01:30:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Combinatorial optimization (CO) problems, central to decision-making scenarios like logistics and manufacturing, are traditionally solved using problem-specific algorithms requiring significant domain expertise. While large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in automating CO problem solving, existing approaches rely on intermediate steps such as code generation or solver invocation, limiting their generality and accessibility. This paper introduces a novel framework that empowers LLMs to serve as end-to-end CO solvers by directly mapping natural language problem descriptions to solutions. We propose a two-stage training strategy: supervised fine-tuning (SFT) imparts LLMs with solution generation patterns from domain-specific solvers, while a feasibility-and-optimality-aware reinforcement learning (FOARL) process explicitly mitigates constraint violations and refines solution quality. Evaluation across seven NP-hard CO problems shows that our method achieves a high feasibility rate and reduces the average optimality gap to 1.03-8.20% by tuning a 7B-parameter LLM, surpassing both general-purpose LLMs (e.g., GPT-4o), reasoning models (e.g., DeepSeek-R1), and domain-specific heuristics. Our method establishes a unified language-based pipeline for CO without extensive code execution or manual architectural adjustments for different problems, offering a general and language-driven alternative to traditional solver design while maintaining relative feasibility guarantees."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:58:24Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:58:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:33:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaehyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Chaehyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heeseong","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Heeseong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunbeen","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Eunbeen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heeji","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Heeji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anurag","familyName":"Arnab","name":"Arnab, Anurag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul Hongsuck","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Paul Hongsuck","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunghwan","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Sunghwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seungryong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seungryong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seg4Diff: Unveiling Open-Vocabulary Segmentation in Text-to-Image Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:59:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:51:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image diffusion models excel at translating language prompts into photorealistic images by implicitly grounding textual concepts through their cross-modal attention mechanisms. Recent multi-modal diffusion transformers extend this by introducing joint self-attention over concatenated image and text tokens, enabling richer and more scalable cross-modal alignment. However, a detailed understanding of how and where these attention maps contribute to image generation remains limited. In this paper, we introduce Seg4Diff (Segmentation for Diffusion), a systematic framework for analyzing the attention structures of MM-DiT, with a focus on how specific layers propagate semantic information from text to image. Through comprehensive analysis, we identify a semantic grounding expert layer, a specific MM-DiT block that consistently aligns text tokens with spatially coherent image regions, naturally producing high-quality semantic segmentation masks. We further demonstrate that applying a lightweight fine-tuning scheme with mask-annotated image data enhances the semantic grouping capabilities of these layers and thereby improves both segmentation performance and generated image fidelity. Our findings demonstrate that semantic grouping is an emergent property of diffusion transformers and can be selectively amplified to advance both segmentation and generation performance, paving the way for unified models that bridge visual perception and generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. Project page: https://cvlab-kaist.github.io/Seg4Diff/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:29Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:27:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Fleshman","name":"Fleshman, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Van Durme","name":"Van Durme, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SEQR: Secure and Efficient QR-based LoRA Routing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:59:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:51:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) has become a standard technique for parameter-efficient fine-tuning of large language models, enabling large libraries of LoRAs, each for a specific task or domain. Efficiently selecting the correct LoRA adapter for a given input remains a challenge, particularly in secure environments where supervised training of routers may raise privacy concerns. Motivated by previous approaches, we formalize the goal of unsupervised LoRA routing in terms of activation norm maximization, providing a theoretical framework for analysis. We demonstrate the discriminative power of activation norms and introduce SEQR, an unsupervised LoRA routing algorithm designed to maximize efficiency while providing strict routing guarantees. SEQR provably identifies the norm-maximizing adapter with significantly greater efficiency, making it a highly scalable and effective solution for dynamic LoRA composition. We validate our results through experiments that demonstrate improved multi-task performance and efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:25Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:27:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunhao","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Sunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakai","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiakai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingjun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bangyang","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Bangyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangle","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kangle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yabo","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Yabo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anxiang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Anxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"See-Kiong","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, See-Kiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OnePiece: Bringing Context Engineering and Reasoning to Industrial Cascade Ranking System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:59:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:51:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite the growing interest in replicating the scaled success of large language models (LLMs) in industrial search and recommender systems, most existing industrial efforts remain limited to transplanting Transformer architectures, which bring only incremental improvements over strong Deep Learning Recommendation Models (DLRMs). From a first principle perspective, the breakthroughs of LLMs stem not only from their architectures but also from two complementary mechanisms: context engineering, which enriches raw input queries with contextual cues to better elicit model capabilities, and multi-step reasoning, which iteratively refines model outputs through intermediate reasoning paths. However, these two mechanisms and their potential to unlock substantial improvements remain largely underexplored in industrial ranking systems.\n In this paper, we propose OnePiece, a unified framework that seamlessly integrates LLM-style context engineering and reasoning into both retrieval and ranking models of industrial cascaded pipelines. OnePiece is built on a pure Transformer backbone and further introduces three key innovations: (1) structured context engineering, which augments interaction history with preference and scenario signals and unifies them into a structured tokenized input sequence for both retrieval and ranking; (2) block-wise latent reasoning, which equips the model with multi-step refinement of representations and scales reasoning bandwidth via block size; (3) progressive multi-task training, which leverages user feedback chains to effectively supervise reasoning steps during training. OnePiece has been deployed in the main personalized search scenario of Shopee and achieves consistent online gains across different key business metrics, including over $+2\\%$ GMV/UU and a $+2.90\\%$ increase in advertising revenue."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"OnePiece Technical Report; Applied in Shopee"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:23Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:27:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18083","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18083","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18083","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Lacombe","name":"Lacombe, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Quesnel","name":"Quesnel, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damien","familyName":"Sileo","name":"Sileo, Damien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Core: A Scalable RL Environment for LLM Symbolic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:56:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:50:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Reasoning Core, a new scalable environment for Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR), designed to advance foundational symbolic reasoning in Large Language Models (LLMs). Unlike existing benchmarks that focus on games or isolated puzzles, Reasoning Core procedurally generates problems across core formal domains, including PDDL planning, first-order logic, context-free grammar parsing, causal reasoning, and system equation solving. The environment is built on key design principles of high-generality problem distributions, verification via external tools, and continuous difficulty control, which together provide a virtually infinite supply of novel training instances. Initial zero-shot evaluations with frontier LLMs confirm the difficulty of Reasoning Core's tasks, positioning it as a promising resource to improve the reasoning capabilities of future models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18083","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:11Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:27:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Mahmudul","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Md. Mahmudul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed Nesar Tahsin","familyName":"Choudhury","name":"Choudhury, Ahmed Nesar Tahsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahmudul","familyName":"Hasan","name":"Hasan, Mahmudul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md. Mosaddek","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Md. Mosaddek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GraDeT-HTR: A Resource-Efficient Bengali Handwritten Text Recognition System utilizing Grapheme-based Tokenizer and Decoder-only Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:56:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:50:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite Bengali being the sixth most spoken language in the world, handwritten text recognition (HTR) systems for Bengali remain severely underdeveloped. The complexity of Bengali script--featuring conjuncts, diacritics, and highly variable handwriting styles--combined with a scarcity of annotated datasets makes this task particularly challenging. We present GraDeT-HTR, a resource-efficient Bengali handwritten text recognition system based on a Grapheme-aware Decoder-only Transformer architecture. To address the unique challenges of Bengali script, we augment the performance of a decoder-only transformer by integrating a grapheme-based tokenizer and demonstrate that it significantly improves recognition accuracy compared to conventional subword tokenizers. Our model is pretrained on large-scale synthetic data and fine-tuned on real human-annotated samples, achieving state-of-the-art performance on multiple benchmark datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages. Accepted at the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP) System Demonstrations. Equal Contribution: Md. Mahmudul Hasan and Ahmed Nesar Tahsin Choudhury"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:09Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:27:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18076","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18076","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18076","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hy","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Hy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuofeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhuofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingfeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Helen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huasheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Huasheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Large Language Models Function Calling and Interpretability via Guided-Structured Templates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:55:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:50:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong reasoning and tool-use capabilities, yet they often fail in real-world tool-interactions due to incorrect parameterization, poor tool selection, or misinterpretation of user intent. These issues often stem from an incomplete understanding of user goals and inadequate comprehension of tool documentation. While Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting has proven effective for enhancing reasoning in general contexts, our analysis reveals that free-form CoT is insufficient and sometimes counterproductive for structured function-calling tasks. To address this, we introduce a curriculum-inspired framework that leverages structured reasoning templates to guide LLMs through more deliberate step-by-step instructions for generating function callings. Experimental results show that our method reduces tool-use errors, achieving 3-12% relative improvements over strong baselines across diverse model series and approaches. Moreover, our framework enhances the robustness, interpretability, and transparency of tool-using agents, advancing the development of more reliable AI assistants for real-world applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18076","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:27:02Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:27:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:27:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18063","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18063","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18063","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jan-Felix","familyName":"Klein","name":"Klein, Jan-Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Ohnemus","name":"Ohnemus, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARK-V1: An LLM-Agent for Knowledge Graph Question Answering Requiring Commonsense Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:40:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:49:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) show strong reasoning abilities but rely on internalized knowledge that is often insufficient, outdated, or incorrect when trying to answer a question that requires specific domain knowledge. Knowledge Graphs (KGs) provide structured external knowledge, yet their complexity and multi-hop reasoning requirements make integration challenging. We present ARK-V1, a simple KG-agent that iteratively explores graphs to answer natural language queries. We evaluate several not fine-tuned state-of-the art LLMs as backbones for ARK-V1 on the CoLoTa dataset, which requires both KG-based and commonsense reasoning over long-tail entities. ARK-V1 achieves substantially higher conditional accuracies than Chain-of-Thought baselines, and larger backbone models show a clear trend toward better coverage, correctness, and stability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in Progess"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18063","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:26:44Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:26:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:26:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Thanh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaozhong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yaozhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Density convergence on Markov diffusion chaos via Stein's method"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60F05, 60H07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:19:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:48:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the difference between the probability density of a random variable $F$ on Markov diffusion chaos and the probability density of a general target distribution $Z$. In the special case where $F$ is a chaotic random variables and $Z$ is a Pearson target, we extend our study to the $k$-th derivatives of the densities for all $k\\in \\mathbb{N}$. In particular, we obtain four moment theorems for the convergence of the $k$-th derivatives of the densities of $F$ to the corresponding $k$-th derivatives of the density of a Pearson target. Our work therefore significantly extends earlier works [HLN14,BDH24] which studies density convergence of random variables on Wiener chaos to respectively the normal and Gamma targets.\n We provide two applications of our results. The first application is about weighted sum of i.i.d. Gamma distribution where we show convergence in laws of this weighted sum to another Gamma distribution automatically implies convergence in densities. In the second application, we show that for a large class of Pearson diffusions, the density of its solution with any initial condition exponentially converge to its limiting density. Moreover, this exponential convergence holds for the $k$-th derivatives of the densities for all $k\\in \\mathbb{N}$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:26:19Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:26:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:26:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Justin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Justin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javid","familyName":"Abderezaei","name":"Abderezaei, Javid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julie","familyName":"Bauml","name":"Bauml, Julie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger","familyName":"Boodoo","name":"Boodoo, Roger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatemeh","familyName":"Haghighi","name":"Haghighi, Fatemeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Ganjizadeh","name":"Ganjizadeh, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Brattain","name":"Brattain, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dave","familyName":"Van Veen","name":"Van Veen, Dave","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zaiqiao","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zaiqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Eyre","name":"Eyre, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean-Benoit","familyName":"Delbrouck","name":"Delbrouck, Jean-Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RadEval: A framework for radiology text evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:03:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:48:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce RadEval, a unified, open-source framework for evaluating radiology texts. RadEval consolidates a diverse range of metrics, from classic n-gram overlap (BLEU, ROUGE) and contextual measures (BERTScore) to clinical concept-based scores (F1CheXbert, F1RadGraph, RaTEScore, SRR-BERT, TemporalEntityF1) and advanced LLM-based evaluators (GREEN). We refine and standardize implementations, extend GREEN to support multiple imaging modalities with a more lightweight model, and pretrain a domain-specific radiology encoder, demonstrating strong zero-shot retrieval performance. We also release a richly annotated expert dataset with over 450 clinically significant error labels and show how different metrics correlate with radiologist judgment. Finally, RadEval provides statistical testing tools and baseline model evaluations across multiple publicly available datasets, facilitating reproducibility and robust benchmarking in radiology report generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Demo track - Oral"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:57Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:25:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18020","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18020","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18020","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunge","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yunge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yibo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alok","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Alok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrés F.","familyName":"Salazar-Gómez","name":"Salazar-Gómez, Andrés F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul Pu","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Paul Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinhua","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jinhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ClassMind: Scaling Classroom Observation and Instructional Feedback with Multimodal AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:59:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:48:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Classroom observation -- one of the most effective methods for teacher development -- remains limited due to high costs and a shortage of expert coaches. We present ClassMind, an AI-driven classroom observation system that integrates generative AI and multimodal learning to analyze classroom artifacts (e.g., class recordings) and deliver timely, personalized feedback aligned with pedagogical practices. At its core is AVA-Align, an agent framework that analyzes long classroom video recordings to generate temporally precise, best-practice-aligned feedback to support teacher reflection and improvement. Our three-phase study involved participatory co-design with educators, development of a full-stack system, and field testing with teachers at different stages of practice. Teachers highlighted the system's usefulness, ease of use, and novelty, while also raising concerns about privacy and the role of human judgment, motivating deeper exploration of future human--AI coaching partnerships. This work illustrates how multimodal AI can scale expert coaching and advance teacher development."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18020","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:43Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:25:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18005","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18005","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18005","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Software Northwestern Polytechnical University"],"givenName":"Yanxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanxin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Software Northwestern Polytechnical University"],"givenName":"Liang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Liang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Software Northwestern Polytechnical University"],"givenName":"Zeyi","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Zeyi","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Laboratoire L2Tl University Sorbonne Paris Nord"],"givenName":"Zuheng","familyName":"Ming","name":"Ming, Zuheng","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["School of Software Yangtze River Delta Research Institute"],"givenName":"Kaixing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kaixing","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"M3ET: Efficient Vision-Language Learning for Robotics based on Multimodal Mamba-Enhanced Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:44:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:47:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, multimodal learning has become essential in robotic vision and information fusion, especially for understanding human behavior in complex environments. However, current methods struggle to fully leverage the textual modality, relying on supervised pretrained models, which limits semantic extraction in unsupervised robotic environments, particularly with significant modality loss. These methods also tend to be computationally intensive, leading to high resource consumption in real-world applications. To address these challenges, we propose the Multi Modal Mamba Enhanced Transformer (M3ET), a lightweight model designed for efficient multimodal learning, particularly on mobile platforms. By incorporating the Mamba module and a semantic-based adaptive attention mechanism, M3ET optimizes feature fusion, alignment, and modality reconstruction. Our experiments show that M3ET improves cross-task performance, with a 2.3 times increase in pretraining inference speed. In particular, the core VQA task accuracy of M3ET remains at 0.74, while the model's parameter count is reduced by 0.67. Although performance on the EQA task is limited, M3ET's lightweight design makes it well suited for deployment on resource-constrained robotic platforms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18005","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:23Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:25:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17985","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17985","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17985","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geonung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Geonung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janghyeok","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Janghyeok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunghyun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Sunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VideoFrom3D: 3D Scene Video Generation via Complementary Image and Video Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:28:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:46:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose VideoFrom3D, a novel framework for synthesizing high-quality 3D scene videos from coarse geometry, a camera trajectory, and a reference image. Our approach streamlines the 3D graphic design workflow, enabling flexible design exploration and rapid production of deliverables. A straightforward approach to synthesizing a video from coarse geometry might condition a video diffusion model on geometric structure. However, existing video diffusion models struggle to generate high-fidelity results for complex scenes due to the difficulty of jointly modeling visual quality, motion, and temporal consistency. To address this, we propose a generative framework that leverages the complementary strengths of image and video diffusion models. Specifically, our framework consists of a Sparse Anchor-view Generation (SAG) and a Geometry-guided Generative Inbetweening (GGI) module. The SAG module generates high-quality, cross-view consistent anchor views using an image diffusion model, aided by Sparse Appearance-guided Sampling. Building on these anchor views, GGI module faithfully interpolates intermediate frames using a video diffusion model, enhanced by flow-based camera control and structural guidance. Notably, both modules operate without any paired dataset of 3D scene models and natural images, which is extremely difficult to obtain. Comprehensive experiments show that our method produces high-quality, style-consistent scene videos under diverse and challenging scenarios, outperforming simple and extended baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://kimgeonung.github.io/VideoFrom3D/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17985","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:24:54Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:24:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:24:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17946","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17946","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17946","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mian","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Mian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pristina","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Pristina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anjalie","familyName":"Field","name":"Field, Anjalie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HICode: Hierarchical Inductive Coding with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:07:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:44:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite numerous applications for fine-grained corpus analysis, researchers continue to rely on manual labeling, which does not scale, or statistical tools like topic modeling, which are difficult to control. We propose that LLMs have the potential to scale the nuanced analyses that researchers typically conduct manually to large text corpora. To this effect, inspired by qualitative research methods, we develop HICode, a two-part pipeline that first inductively generates labels directly from analysis data and then hierarchically clusters them to surface emergent themes. We validate this approach across three diverse datasets by measuring alignment with human-constructed themes and demonstrating its robustness through automated and human evaluations. Finally, we conduct a case study of litigation documents related to the ongoing opioid crisis in the U.S., revealing aggressive marketing strategies employed by pharmaceutical companies and demonstrating HICode's potential for facilitating nuanced analyses in large-scale data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Long paper accepted at EMNLP 2025 main conference, 19 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17946","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:59Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:24:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:24:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Romain","familyName":"Thoreau","name":"Thoreau, Romain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessie","familyName":"Levillain","name":"Levillain, Jessie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawa","familyName":"Derksen","name":"Derksen, Dawa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can multimodal representation learning by alignment preserve modality-specific information?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:06:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:44:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Combining multimodal data is a key issue in a wide range of machine learning tasks, including many remote sensing problems. In Earth observation, early multimodal data fusion methods were based on specific neural network architectures and supervised learning. Ever since, the scarcity of labeled data has motivated self-supervised learning techniques. State-of-the-art multimodal representation learning techniques leverage the spatial alignment between satellite data from different modalities acquired over the same geographic area in order to foster a semantic alignment in the latent space. In this paper, we investigate how this methods can preserve task-relevant information that is not shared across modalities. First, we show, under simplifying assumptions, when alignment strategies fundamentally lead to an information loss. Then, we support our theoretical insight through numerical experiments in more realistic settings. With those theoretical and empirical evidences, we hope to support new developments in contrastive learning for the combination of multimodal satellite data. Our code and data is publicly available at https://github.com/Romain3Ch216/alg_maclean_25."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a workshop paper at MACLEAN - ECML/PKDD 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:55Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:23:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:23:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17941","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17941","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17941","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Munje","name":"Munje, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuijing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garrett","familyName":"Warnell","name":"Warnell, Garrett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Stone","name":"Stone, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joydeep","familyName":"Biswas","name":"Biswas, Joydeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ComposableNav: Instruction-Following Navigation in Dynamic Environments via Composable Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:04:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:44:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper considers the problem of enabling robots to navigate dynamic environments while following instructions. The challenge lies in the combinatorial nature of instruction specifications: each instruction can include multiple specifications, and the number of possible specification combinations grows exponentially as the robot's skill set expands. For example, \"overtake the pedestrian while staying on the right side of the road\" consists of two specifications: \"overtake the pedestrian\" and \"walk on the right side of the road.\" To tackle this challenge, we propose ComposableNav, based on the intuition that following an instruction involves independently satisfying its constituent specifications, each corresponding to a distinct motion primitive. Using diffusion models, ComposableNav learns each primitive separately, then composes them in parallel at deployment time to satisfy novel combinations of specifications unseen in training. Additionally, to avoid the onerous need for demonstrations of individual motion primitives, we propose a two-stage training procedure: (1) supervised pre-training to learn a base diffusion model for dynamic navigation, and (2) reinforcement learning fine-tuning that molds the base model into different motion primitives. Through simulation and real-world experiments, we show that ComposableNav enables robots to follow instructions by generating trajectories that satisfy diverse and unseen combinations of specifications, significantly outperforming both non-compositional VLM-based policies and costmap composing baselines. Videos and additional materials can be found on the project page: https://amrl.cs.utexas.edu/ComposableNav/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Conference on Robot Learning (CoRL) 2025 Project site: https://amrl.cs.utexas.edu/ComposableNav/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17941","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:52Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:23:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:23:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17938","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17938","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17938","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satyapriya","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Satyapriya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andy","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Andy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliot Krzysztof","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Eliot Krzysztof","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Winter","name":"Winter, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Hendrycks","name":"Hendrycks, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. Zico","familyName":"Kolter","name":"Kolter, J. Zico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt","familyName":"Fredrikson","name":"Fredrikson, Matt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Spyros","familyName":"Matsoukas","name":"Matsoukas, Spyros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"D-REX: A Benchmark for Detecting Deceptive Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:59:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:44:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The safety and alignment of Large Language Models (LLMs) are critical for their responsible deployment. Current evaluation methods predominantly focus on identifying and preventing overtly harmful outputs. However, they often fail to address a more insidious failure mode: models that produce benign-appearing outputs while operating on malicious or deceptive internal reasoning. This vulnerability, often triggered by sophisticated system prompt injections, allows models to bypass conventional safety filters, posing a significant, underexplored risk. To address this gap, we introduce the Deceptive Reasoning Exposure Suite (D-REX), a novel dataset designed to evaluate the discrepancy between a model's internal reasoning process and its final output. D-REX was constructed through a competitive red-teaming exercise where participants crafted adversarial system prompts to induce such deceptive behaviors. Each sample in D-REX contains the adversarial system prompt, an end-user's test query, the model's seemingly innocuous response, and, crucially, the model's internal chain-of-thought, which reveals the underlying malicious intent. Our benchmark facilitates a new, essential evaluation task: the detection of deceptive alignment. We demonstrate that D-REX presents a significant challenge for existing models and safety mechanisms, highlighting the urgent need for new techniques that scrutinize the internal processes of LLMs, not just their final outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17938","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:48Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:23:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:23:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17917","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17917","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17917","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Junyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Songxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zejian","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zejian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoyang","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhuoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Orcust: Stepwise-Feedback Reinforcement Learning for GUI Agent"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:40:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in GUI agents have achieved remarkable grounding and action-prediction performance, yet existing models struggle with unreliable reward signals and limited online trajectory generation. In this paper, we introduce Orcust, a framework that integrates Principle-Constrained Reward Modeling (PCRM) and Online VM-Grounded Trajectory Construction (OVTC) to enhance reasoning reliability and data efficiency in interactive GUI tasks. We leverages environment-verifiable and LLM-derived principle to enforce interpretable reward signals that constrain long chain-of-thought reasoning and rule-based feedback. OVTC spins up instrumented virtual machines to autonomously collect structured GUI interaction trajectories with explicit procedural and structural objectives, enabling the training of a stepwise reward model that robustly captures human preferences and adheres to task-specific constraints. Extensive experiments on standard GUI benchmarks covering perceptual grounding, foundational operations, and end-to-end task execution reveal that Orcust achieves state-of-the-art performance, improving by 22.2\\% on ScreenSpot and 23.9\\% on ScreenSpot-Pro over the base model (i.e. Qwen2.5-VL-7B). The results demonstrate Orcust's effectiveness in enhancing the reasoning, adaptability and scalability of GUI agents across various environments and task complexities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17917","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:19Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:23:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:23:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcin","familyName":"Copik","name":"Copik, Marcin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eiman","familyName":"Alnuaimi","name":"Alnuaimi, Eiman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alok","familyName":"Kamatar","name":"Kamatar, Alok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valerie","familyName":"Hayot-Sasson","name":"Hayot-Sasson, Valerie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Madonna","name":"Madonna, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Todd","familyName":"Gamblin","name":"Gamblin, Todd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Chard","name":"Chard, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Foster","name":"Foster, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Torsten","familyName":"Hoefler","name":"Hoefler, Torsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"XaaS Containers: Performance-Portable Representation With Source and IR Containers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:39:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:42:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3712285.3759868","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-performance computing (HPC) systems and cloud data centers are converging, and containers are becoming the default method of portable software deployment. Yet, while containers simplify software management, they face significant performance challenges in HPC environments as they must sacrifice hardware-specific optimizations to achieve portability. Although HPC containers can use runtime hooks to access optimized MPI libraries and GPU devices, they are limited by application binary interface (ABI) compatibility and cannot overcome the effects of early-stage compilation decisions. Acceleration as a Service (XaaS) proposes a vision of performance-portable containers, where a containerized application should achieve peak performance across all HPC systems. We present a practical realization of this vision through Source and Intermediate Representation (IR) containers, where we delay performance-critical decisions until the target system specification is known. We analyze specialization mechanisms in HPC software and propose a new LLM-assisted method for automatic discovery of specializations. By examining the compilation pipeline, we develop a methodology to build containers optimized for target architectures at deployment time. Our prototype demonstrates that new XaaS containers combine the convenience of containerization with the performance benefits of system-specialized builds."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the International Conference for High Performance Computing, Networking, Storage and Analysis (SC'25)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T03:23:14Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:23:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:23:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divya","familyName":"Mereddy","name":"Mereddy, Divya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcos","familyName":"Quinones-Grueiro","name":"Quinones-Grueiro, Marcos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwin T","familyName":"S","name":"S, Ashwin T","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo","familyName":"Davalos","name":"Davalos, Eduardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautam","familyName":"Biswas","name":"Biswas, Gautam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kent","familyName":"Etherton","name":"Etherton, Kent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katelyn","familyName":"Kay","name":"Kay, Katelyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jill","familyName":"Lear","name":"Lear, Jill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Goldberg","name":"Goldberg, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trainee Action Recognition through Interaction Analysis in CCATT Mixed-Reality Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:19:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:41:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study examines how Critical Care Air Transport Team (CCATT) members are trained using mixed-reality simulations that replicate the high-pressure conditions of aeromedical evacuation. Each team - a physician, nurse, and respiratory therapist - must stabilize severely injured soldiers by managing ventilators, IV pumps, and suction devices during flight. Proficient performance requires clinical expertise and cognitive skills, such as situational awareness, rapid decision-making, effective communication, and coordinated task management, all of which must be maintained under stress. Recent advances in simulation and multimodal data analytics enable more objective and comprehensive performance evaluation. In contrast, traditional instructor-led assessments are subjective and may overlook critical events, thereby limiting generalizability and consistency. However, AI-based automated and more objective evaluation metrics still demand human input to train the AI algorithms to assess complex team dynamics in the presence of environmental noise and the need for accurate re-identification in multi-person tracking. To address these challenges, we introduce a systematic, data-driven assessment framework that combines Cognitive Task Analysis (CTA) with Multimodal Learning Analytics (MMLA). We have developed a domain-specific CTA model for CCATT training and a vision-based action recognition pipeline using a fine-tuned Human-Object Interaction model, the Cascade Disentangling Network (CDN), to detect and track trainee-equipment interactions over time. These interactions automatically yield performance indicators (e.g., reaction time, task duration), which are mapped onto a hierarchical CTA model tailored to CCATT operations, enabling interpretable, domain-relevant performance evaluations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:22:38Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:22:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:22:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17879","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17879","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17879","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tu","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander Miserlis","familyName":"Hoyle","name":"Hoyle, Alexander Miserlis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Cotterell","name":"Cotterell, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Persuasive is Your Context?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T15:15:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:40:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Two central capabilities of language models (LMs) are: (i) drawing on prior knowledge about entities, which allows them to answer queries such as \"What's the official language of Austria?\", and (ii) adapting to new information provided in context, e.g., \"Pretend the official language of Austria is Tagalog.\", that is pre-pended to the question. In this article, we introduce targeted persuasion score (TPS), designed to quantify how persuasive a given context is to an LM where persuasion is operationalized as the ability of the context to alter the LM's answer to the question. In contrast to evaluating persuasiveness only by inspecting the greedily decoded answer under the model, TPS provides a more fine-grained view of model behavior. Based on the Wasserstein distance, TPS measures how much a context shifts a model's original answer distribution toward a target distribution. Empirically, through a series of experiments, we show that TPS captures a more nuanced notion of persuasiveness than previously proposed metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Long paper accepted at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17879","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:22:25Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:22:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:22:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17863","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17863","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17863","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyu","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Boyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoteng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guoteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaohe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanmin","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yanmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingjun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiaoling","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qiaoling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenggan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Shenggan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"You","name":"You, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Expert-as-a-Service: Towards Efficient, Scalable, and Robust Large-scale MoE Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T14:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:40:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models challenge serving infrastructures with dynamic, sparse expert utilization, causing instability on conventional systems designed for dense architectures. We propose EaaS, a novel serving system to enable efficient, scalable, and robust MoE deployment. Our system disaggregates MoE modules into independent, stateless services. This design enables fine-grained resource scaling and provides inherent fault tolerance by decoupling compute units. The architecture is powered by a high-performance, CPU-free peer-to-peer communication library that ensures minimal overhead and high throughput. Experiments confirm EaaS's scalability and efficiency, achieving performance comparable to monolithic systems while providing robust fault tolerance and strong scalability. EaaS incurs less than a 2% throughput reduction under simulated hardware failures that would otherwise halt monolithic architectures. It further saves up to 37.5% of computing resources through dynamic fine-grained adaptation to serving traffic, demonstrating strong resilience for large-scale MoE deployment in production."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17863","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:22:02Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:22:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:22:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17855","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17855","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17855","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Litschko","name":"Litschko, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Verena","familyName":"Blaschke","name":"Blaschke, Verena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diana","familyName":"Burkhardt","name":"Burkhardt, Diana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barbara","familyName":"Plank","name":"Plank, Barbara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diego","familyName":"Frassinelli","name":"Frassinelli, Diego","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Make Every Letter Count: Building Dialect Variation Dictionaries from Monolingual Corpora"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T14:49:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:39:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dialects exhibit a substantial degree of variation due to the lack of a standard orthography. At the same time, the ability of Large Language Models (LLMs) to process dialects remains largely understudied. To address this gap, we use Bavarian as a case study and investigate the lexical dialect understanding capability of LLMs by examining how well they recognize and translate dialectal terms across different parts-of-speech. To this end, we introduce DiaLemma, a novel annotation framework for creating dialect variation dictionaries from monolingual data only, and use it to compile a ground truth dataset consisting of 100K human-annotated German-Bavarian word pairs. We evaluate how well nine state-of-the-art LLMs can judge Bavarian terms as dialect translations, inflected variants, or unrelated forms of a given German lemma. Our results show that LLMs perform best on nouns and lexically similar word pairs, and struggle most in distinguishing between direct translations and inflected variants. Interestingly, providing additional context in the form of example usages improves the translation performance, but reduces their ability to recognize dialect variants. This study highlights the limitations of LLMs in dealing with orthographic dialect variation and emphasizes the need for future work on adapting LLMs to dialects."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17855","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:21:51Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:21:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:21:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":440,"totalPages":18,"page":9},"links":{"self":"https://